digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56394-9
  rankdir="TB";
  "N102" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: If existing CPL materials have fundamental limitations (high cost, poor stability, low performance), then these limitations create a research gap."];
  "N105" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Common pattern: 0D structures show g_lum ~10⁻⁴ to 10⁻², 1D structures show g_lum ~10⁻³ to 10⁻²."];
  "N106" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: If structural dimension affects g_lum AND we can control structural dimension, then we can systematically optimize g_lum."];
  "N108" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: If the mismatch between μ and m causes low g_lum AND structural dimension can balance μ and m, then controlling structural dimension can improve g_lum."];
  "N110" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: If lead-free metal halide hybrids have advantages (low cost, high PLQY, diverse structures) AND can achieve CPL through CISS effect, then they are promising platforms for CPL optimization."];
  "N112" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: If no systematic rules govern CPL performance AND structural dimension shows correlation with g_lum, then establishing structure-property relationships through dimensional control could provide design rules."];
  "N116" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Chiral organic cations with hydrogen-bonding acceptors can induce chirality transfer in metal halides."];
  "N117" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Common mechanism: Asymmetric hydrogen bonds → lattice distortion → excited state deformation → STEs → efficient emission with chirality."];
  "N118" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: If tuning chiral organic cations with H-bonding acceptors enhances chirality transfer AND coordinating with K⁺ ions further enhances this transfer, then using K⁺-coordinated chiral cations can maximize chirality transfer across dimensions."];
  "N12" [label="(12,0,0) The CISS effect endows lead-free metal halide hybrids with more stable intrinsic chirality and excellent CPL behavior, positioning them as promising high-performance CPL emitter candidates."];
  "N120" [label="(0,0,0) Abduction: If high PLQY and large g_lum are contradictory AND structural dimension can balance μ and m values, then dimensional regulation can overcome the PLQY-g_lum contradiction."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) Asymmetric hydrogen bonds between chiral organic cations and halide ions exacerbate lattice distortion, leading to excited state deformation."];
  "N15" [label="(15,0,0) Deformed excited state induces self-trapped excitons (STEs) generation, initiating efficient STEs emission and exhibiting chiral luminescent properties."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) Several significant achievements have been realized in chiral lead-free metal halides."];
  "N20" [label="(20,0,0) The g_lum for 0D (R/S-C₆H₁₅ClNO)₂•SbCl₅ is ±3.0×10⁻⁴, while for 1D (D/L)-(tert-butylprolinate)MnCl₃ is ±6.0×10⁻³."];
  "N22" [label="(22,0,0) Despite progress, CPL performance is not governed by specific rules and is typically obtained through trial-and-error approach."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Generally |μ| >> |m|, leading to mismatch between electric and magnetic transition dipole moments, resulting in g_lum around 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻³."];
  "N6" [label="(6,0,0) The design challenge lies in balancing g_lum and PLQY via modulating both value and orientation of μ and m."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) Most existing CPL-active materials are barely promising for brilliant CPL emitters due to high cost, poor thermal stability, and low luminescence intensity."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This work aims to develop and evaluate lead-free indium-based chiral metal-halide hybrids by rationally tuning structural dimensionality from 0D toward 3D using highly polar chiral organic cations and K⁺/Sb³⁺-associated hydrogen-bond networks, to strengthen chirality transfer, balance electric and magnetic transition dipoles, and pursue simultaneous high photoluminescence quantum yield, large circularly polarized luminescence dissymmetry factors, and improved figure of merit.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A clean paper-level synthesis should connect material limitations, lead-free chiral metal-halide platform advantages, dimensional control, hydrogen-bond/K⁺-assisted chirality transfer, electric/magnetic dipole balancing, and PLQY–g_lum/FOM optimization into one cautious research aim."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because CPL performance is often obtained by trial and error, clarifying structure-property relationships through dimensional control could provide useful design guidance."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Achieving bright CPL requires balancing high PLQY with large g_lum, and dimensional regulation may help address this challenge by modulating electric and magnetic transition dipoles."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the evidence motivates developing and evaluating a dimensional-regulation strategy for chiral lead-free metal-halide hybrids that combines controllable structural dimensionality, chiral-cation hydrogen bonding/K⁺ coordination, dipole balancing, and PLQY–g_lum figure-of-merit optimization."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Reported 0D and 1D metal-halide examples show dimension-associated but overlapping g_lum ranges, motivating cautious study of how structural dimensionality relates to CPL dissymmetry."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because reported metal-halide examples show dimension-associated variation in g_lum and structural dimension can in principle be controlled, systematic dimensional regulation is a plausible route to investigate g_lum optimization."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The hydrogen-bond-mediated chirality-transfer mechanism supports testing K⁺-coordinated chiral cations as a design strategy that may further enhance chirality transfer."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Limitations of many existing CPL-active materials, including cost, stability, and emission-intensity issues, create a research gap for improved CPL emitters."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) The CISS effect and direct incorporation of chiral organic cations into lead-free metal-halide lattices support treating lead-free metal-halide hybrids as promising platforms for CPL optimization."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Mismatch between electric and magnetic transition dipole moments helps explain low g_lum values, so structural approaches that balance these dipoles are plausible routes for improving CPL dissymmetry."];
  "N14" -> "N117" [label="induction-case"];
  "N15" -> "N117" [label="induction-case"];
  "N16" -> "N117" [label="induction-case"];
